# Responsible use

DriftSense Pilot is a research prototype built for the OpenCV AI Competition.
It decides where a microscope should look and whether a stage move is safe; a
wrong decision means measuring the wrong structure. These are the limits we
designed for and the ways we expect it to be used.

## Synthetic data only

- Every quantitative result is computed on images from our virtual SEM
  (`driftsense.sim`, documented in [virtual_sem.md](virtual_sem.md)): synthetic
  line-edge roughness, shot noise, drift and distortion on generated layouts.
- No customer, fab or proprietary data was used to build, tune or evaluate the
  system, and no real-tool images are part of the evaluation.
- The generator encodes our assumptions about SEM physics. Results on it do not
  transfer automatically to a real instrument.

## A human approves uncertain stage moves

- The agent commits a stage move only when the uniqueness margin clears a
  threshold calibrated on a separate calibration split. The runner re-checks
  that condition itself; no policy, rule-based or LLM, can override it.
- When it cannot prove uniqueness within its step and dose budget, it stops and
  asks for human approval, showing the top candidates and the evidence behind
  each. Rejecting the card means no stage move.
- The optional LLM planner can only propose perception tools (acquire,
  localize, refine). It can never commit or escalate on its own.
- Every step, decision and approval is written to a JSONL trace with the margin
  and threshold that justified it.

## Dose limits

- Re-acquisition (longer dwell, wider field of view) adds electron dose, which
  can damage sensitive resists. The runner has a small fixed cap on decision
  steps and accepts a per-episode dose budget; with a budget configured, a
  projected or instrument-raised overrun escalates to a human rather than
  acquiring more.
{% if exists("sealed/decisions", "rules.D12_agent_budget.pass") and flag("sealed/decisions", "rules.D12_agent_budget.pass") == false %}
- In the sealed evaluation the agent's mean dose exceeded the pre-registered
  budget and the corresponding decision rule failed (see
  [SUBMISSION.md](../SUBMISSION.md)). A deployment must configure the budget so
  the runner enforces it.
{% endif %}

## Data retention and security

- Uploaded images, job records and traces are kept for seven days and then
  deleted by an S3 lifecycle rule; curated demo inputs are kept. Buckets are
  private, encrypted and TLS-only; uploads use short-lived presigned URLs with a
  size cap.
- The worker has no inbound network access and no SSH keys; administration is
  through AWS Systems Manager only. Deployment uses GitHub OIDC, not stored keys.

## Not validated on production tools

- DriftSense Pilot has not been validated on a production SEM or in a fab. Do
  not use it to make process-control decisions without an independent
  qualification on the target tool, recipe and layout.
- The DRAM contour stage is not implemented in this build; contact arrays go
  through the line-roughness path.

## Honest reporting

Every number in these documents is rendered from versioned results files by
`scripts/render_docs.py` and checked by `scripts/check_claims.py`. Development
numbers are labelled DEV; only the pre-registered, hash-locked evaluation is
labelled SEALED, and its failed decision rules are reported as failed.
